15:16:05 | INFO | 🤖 Target initial answer: It’s a great question, and the short answer is: we’ve already technically “run out” of new IPv4 addresses, and the internet didn’t break. Instead, the world has been adapting for years using a mix of workarounds, a slow-but-steady migration to IPv6, and an active market for the remaining IPv4 blocks. Here’s what actually happens in practice. ### 1. The exhaustion already happened (in stages) - In 2011, **IANA** (the global top‑level authority) allocated its last blocks of fresh IPv4 space to the five Regional Internet Registries (RIRs). - By 2015, all RIRs had exhausted their general‑use pools (APNIC first, then RIPE NCC, ARIN, etc.), though some still hand out tiny recovered blocks or maintain waiting lists. So when people say “we’ve run out”, they mean no *new* provider can get a large, previously unused IPv4 block from the official supply. ### 2. NAT became the universal band‑aid Network Address Translation (NAT) lets hundreds of devices share a single public IPv4 address. Most home and office networks already do this behind a router. When the public pools dried up, ISPs turned to **Carrier‑Grade NAT (CGNAT)** — putting thousands of subscribers behind a shared pool of IPv4 addresses. - **Pros:** Almost all consumer internet use (web browsing, streaming, social media) works fine. - **Cons:** Sometimes you get a shared IP reputation (if someone else is blocked, you might be too), hosting services at home becomes harder, and certain peer‑to‑peer apps or online gaming may have trouble. ### 3. The IPv6 transition accelerated IPv6 has an astronomically large address space (340 undecillion addresses), so it’s the real long‑term solution. What we’re seeing: - **Dual stack:** Networks run IPv4 and IPv6 side by side. Many large content providers (Google, YouTube, Netflix, Facebook) are fully reachable over IPv6. - **NAT64/DNS64:** Mobile carriers (T‑Mobile, Reliance Jio, etc.) largely moved to IPv6‑only networks. When you need to reach an IPv4‑only site, the carrier’s NAT64 gateway translates between IPv6 and IPv4 automatically. To your phone, it’s seamless. - **Home users:** Most modern consumer routers and operating systems have been IPv6‑capable for over a decade. The biggest hurdle is ISP deployment, but adoption is growing steadily (currently around 40–50% global IPv6 traffic share depending on region). ### 4. IPv4 addresses became a commodity With no free pool left, IPv4 addresses are now bought, sold, and leased on a transfer market. - Prices have risen from a few dollars per address in the 2010s to around $40–60 per address today (and sometimes higher for clean, reputable blocks). - Companies that need public IPv4 space — hosting providers, cloud services, new ISPs — must purchase blocks from organizations willing to sell. This adds cost to infrastructure, which can trickle down to customers. ### 5. Real‑world impact for different groups - **Average residential user:** You probably don’t notice. NAT and IPv6 handle almost everything. The only occasional hiccup is when a service strictly requires a unique public IPv4 (like some VPN or remote desktop setups), but those are becoming rarer. - **Businesses and hosting:** They feel the squeeze. Running your own mail server, game server, or exposing a service to the internet often still needs a dedicated IPv4 — and that now costs money. Many startups just go IPv6‑first with a NAT64 gateway for legacy IPv4 access. - **Developing regions:** Some newer ISPs in countries that obtained fewer IPv4 addresses historically are more aggressively using CGNAT and IPv6, which can create performance quirks or content‑delivery challenges if IPv6 isn’t fully deployed by the sites their users visit. ### 6. No “internet meltdown” — just a slow, messy coexistence The internet isn’t going to suddenly stop working. We’ve been running in this post‑exhaustion state for nearly a decade. The reality is: - IPv4 will be around for a very long time as a legacy protocol, but it will gradually become a “secondary” or “limited” service. - New devices, cloud instances, and services will increasingly be IPv6‑only (or IPv6‑first). - Over time, the pain points from CGNAT and IPv4 scarcity will push more networks and content to fully embrace IPv6, but it’s a decades‑long process akin to switching from analog TV to digital. In short: running out of IPv4 addresses didn’t cause a catastrophe; it forced the global internet to grow up, get creative with sharing, and finally take IPv6 seriously. The end user experience has stayed mostly seamless, while behind the scenes engineers trade and share the dwindling IPv4 resource like a scarce digital good.
