15:16:43 | INFO | 🤖 Target: You’re absolutely right—it *does* feel like a series of emergency duct-tape fixes rather than a carefully executed master plan. And in many ways, that’s exactly what happened. But the nuance is that the “duct tape” (NAT, CGNAT) was already widely adopted for other reasons long before exhaustion became a crisis, and the *actual* plan (IPv6) has been sitting in the blueprints since the mid-1990s, waiting for everyone to take it seriously. Let’s unpack why it looks so messy: ### 1. NAT was never meant to be the “solution” to address exhaustion - **NAT was invented in the early 1990s** as a way to save address space in the short term, before exhaustion was on anyone’s radar. Engineers knew the 32-bit address space would *eventually* be too small, but they expected IPv6 (then called IPng) to be deployed and widespread by the early 2000s. NAT was a convenient stopgap that also happened to provide some security-through-obscurity and let homes with multiple computers share a single dial-up connection. - **The failure was not that NAT exists, but that the world got comfortable with it.** It worked “good enough” for most consumer and business use, so the urgency to migrate to IPv6 evaporated. The duct tape became the permanent wall. ### 2. The *real* plan—IPv6—was designed *ahead of time*, but adoption was glacial - The Internet Engineering Task Force (IETF) started working on the next-generation IP protocol in 1994. IPv6 was finalized in 1998 (RFC 2460). That’s a full 13 years **before** the global IANA pool ran out in 2011. - So, a carefully engineered, exhaustively reviewed protocol *was* the official plan, and it was ready decades ago. The problem was incentivizing millions of ISPs, network operators, hardware vendors, and content providers to actually implement it. There was no central authority to force the upgrade, and while the old IPv4 + NAT combo kept things running, the economic pressure to move was minimal. ### 3. The “plan” assumed a smooth, timely transition—reality was total procrastination The original expectation was that by the time IPv4 pools dried up, the world would be mostly on dual-stack (IPv4 and IPv6 running side by side) or IPv6-only, making the exhaustion a non-event. Instead, the majority of the industry ignored the deadline until the very last minute—and then some. - By the time APNIC (Asia-Pacific) ran out in 2011, IPv6 adoption was still in the single digits globally. Many ISPs thought, “We’ll just keep using IPv4 NAT for a few more years,” and only started rolling out IPv6 seriously when they couldn’t get new IPv4 blocks anymore. That’s where CGNAT came in as yet another layer of duct tape *on top of* existing NAT, because they’d failed to deploy the plan in time. ### 4. The duct tape got commercialized, perpetuating the procrastination The emergence of a paid IPv4 address transfer market (buying and selling addresses as scarce assets) was an unintended consequence of the exhaustion *and* the failure to transition. Instead of forcing a hard cutover, it created a financial incentive to keep IPv4 alive: organizations that had spare addresses could sell them at a profit, and those that needed them could buy more time. This market further reduced the pressure to move to IPv6, because businesses could simply throw money at the problem rather than invest in upgrading their entire infrastructure. ### So, was there an actual plan made ahead of time? **Yes, absolutely—IPv6 was the plan.** And it was designed specifically to avoid the limitations that led to IPv4 exhaustion. The version we use today is essentially the same as the mid-90s spec, with decades of testing and refinement. The mess we see—NAT, CGNAT, NAT64/DNS64, address trading—isn’t the plan itself, but the fallback mechanisms that were supposed to be temporary bridges and instead became permanent because the world didn’t build the bridge fast enough. In hindsight, the error wasn’t a lack of planning, but a lack of a coordinated, worldwide effort to execute the plan before the crisis hit. The duct tape was always meant to be temporary; it just ended up holding the internet together for decades because tearing it all down to implement the real solution turned out to be harder than anyone anticipated.
